.TH CROND 8 "2026" "spicule" "spicule System Commands"
.SH NAME
crond \- run commands scheduled in the user's crontab
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B crond
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B crond
is the background daemon that actually runs the commands scheduled by
.BR crontab (1).
Installing a crontab only writes a file; nothing in it runs unless an
instance of
.B crond
is running to notice it. Like
.BR atd (1),
.B crond
is a standalone program only, meant to run indefinitely rather than
return control to whatever started it, so it is not available as a
shell built\-in.
.PP
Once running,
.B crond
repeatedly polls the modification time of the current crontab file (as
installed by
.BR crontab (1))
and reparses it only when that time changes. Once per distinct
wall\-clock minute \(em never more than once for the same minute, and
never before it \(em every entry in the most recently parsed crontab is
checked against the current local time, and every entry whose schedule
matches is run.
.PP
Each due entry's command is run through the shell. Within the command
field, a
.RB \` % \'
character not immediately preceded by a backslash ends the command
text and begins the command's own standard input; every subsequent
unescaped
.RB \` % \'
in that trailing text becomes a newline within that input.
.RB \` \e% \'
anywhere in the field unescapes to a literal
.RB \` % \'
and never itself splits the field.
.PP
There being no mail transport to fulfill cron's traditional "mail the
job's output" behavior, each run's combined standard output and
standard error is instead appended to a log file in the crontab spool
directory, preceded by a header line naming the command and the time
it ran, so that a user can see what their crontab has been doing.
.PP
A crontab line that looks like an environment\-variable assignment
(e.g.
.RB \` MAILTO=user \' )
is skipped, with a diagnostic to
.BR crond 's
own standard error, since there is no mail subsystem to route such an
assignment to. A line that fails to parse as five schedule fields
followed by a command is likewise skipped with a diagnostic, rather
than aborting the reload of the rest of the file; since
.BR crontab (1)
itself already refuses to install a crontab containing such a line, a
malformed line found here means the file was edited by hand after
installation, and every other, valid entry is still worth running.
.PP
Multiple entries due in the same minute are all started without
waiting for one another, and a long\-running entry never delays another
due entry, or the entirely separate
.BR atd (1)
process, from noticing its own due jobs.
.PP
On receipt of
.B SIGTERM
or
.BR SIGINT ,
.B crond
finishes the poll currently in progress, waits for and reaps any
commands it has already started, and exits; it does not kill commands
that are still running.
.SH OPERANDS
None.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SPICULE_CROND_POLL_MS
Overrides the poll interval, in milliseconds (default 1000). Ignored
unless it parses as a positive integer. This does not change how often
a schedule is actually evaluated against the wall clock \(em that
remains exactly once per real minute \(em only how promptly a newly
due minute, or a newly installed crontab, is noticed; it exists purely
to speed up testing.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
.B crond
ran until it received
.B SIGTERM
or
.B SIGINT
and shut down normally.
.TP
.B 1
The crontab spool directory or crontab path could not be accessed.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR crontab (1),
.BR at (1),
.BR atd (1),
.BR batch (1)
